/-!
# Arithmetic Hodge Theory of Degenerations

Umbrella for the arithmetic Hodge theory of the degeneration of the scaling surface along the
resolution axis (overlap `c`, horizon `N`). See `ArithmeticHodgeTheoryOfDegenerations/README.md`.

* **First order.** The rescaled limit is a count: `J(m) = J(1) #ℙ⁷(ℤ/m)`; the count law holds over
  every finite ring.
* **Zero ledgers.** A point of the strip enters a fixed packet only through a positive pole weight;
  uniformly below the grid `λN` of the conductor polynomials (`2λL ≤ π`), far above the resolution
  height `N/c`, the zeros are blind to pole-free packets and give the archimedean genus
  `κ y₁²(2 + γ − log 4π)` at the pole. Horizon windows read Weil's summands;
  twisted windows read no zero.
* **Bulk second order.** Since `Λ ∗ 1 = log`, the prime matrix pairs conductor packets through
  harmonic and logarithmic harmonic sums; every bulk entry has second order
  `⟨P R_e, R_{e'}⟩ − (log N − ρ_e)⟨R_e, R_{e'}⟩ → D(e, e')`, a polynomial in `γ`, `γ₁` and the
  logarithms of divisors (`D(1,1) = 2γ₁ + γ²`, `D(p,p) = D(1,1) + p log²p/(p − 1)²`). At every
  overlap `0 < c < 1` the complete entry adds the center part (`α(1 − log 2)` at the pole,
  `αΛ(e)/φ(e)` on the diagonal) and the cross error of the archimedean smoothing. Along a growing
  overlap `c → ∞`, `c² log² N/N → 0`, the entry minus `E_e(c)⟨R_e, R_{e'}⟩` is
  `[e = e' = 1] κ c (2 + γ − log 4π) + o(c)`: the logarithms `κ c log c` of the shells, the near
  center and the near pairs cancel, as do the divisor jets and the center constants; on the tilted
  spline, the critical curve `c⁸ ≍ K log N` adds `K κ (2 + γ − log 4π)` at the pole to the Jordan
  limit. Character packets
  `χ(n)/√n` sit at the bounded energy `−2 Re L'(1,χ)/L(1,χ)` with second order
  `−2(φ(q)/q) Re (L'/L)'(1, χ)`.
* **Second order at the critical curve.** On horizon windows `κ Q(H) + (βJ/K) ∫ e^{−(β+1)y} H²`,
  the aliasing being the dilation derivative of the bulk; on twisted windows a spectral Dirichlet
  series: Jordan totients for Ramanujan expansions (graded by conductor), the unit count corrected
  by `L(β+1, ψ̄²)` for characters (graded by parity, equal parities coupled by `L`-values), and the
  Hurwitz form `½(ζ(β+1,u) + ζ(β+1,1−u))` for a frequency `u`, rational or irrational, the layer
  being graded by frequency (trigonometric twists carry `Σ a_k²` times these forms); the frequency
  counts have a pole of order `β + 1` at zero frequency whose finite part is the untwisted count.
* **Critical boundary.** On the canonical tilted-spline clock, every independent fixed window
  family has eventual inertia `(n₊(P), n₋(P)+n₀(P), 0)`: the negative inverse-square correction
  makes every null direction negative. The limiting critical ratio alone permits either sign.
* **The integer.** The window index of the pencil `κ W(H) + (βJ/K) A(H)`: read by the complete
  family, counting the right zeros, jumping at the critical multiples, between the resolved right
  zeros and the Shannon number; for `β > 0`, `0` below one positive multiple independent of
  aperture (the exponential weighted Weil floor), `0` on windows of
  length `≤ 3/5`, `≤ 1` on smooth windows of length `< log 2 + 1/50` (cosh hyperplane).
* **Mechanism B.** Inheritance through the critical curve on every twist with a positive scalar
  second order (every periodic twist, every trigonometric twist with distinct frequencies), on
  short windows, on the cosh hyperplane of the first-prime windows, on all windows below one
  aperture-independent positive multiple,
  and on mixed families.
* **Calibration over `𝔽_q`.** Lattice tests are diagonal in the Frobenius angles; the rescaled limit
  is their Hurwitz form; the family is stationary along the horizon; a single orbit `β, β⁻¹` dilates
  by a scalar; a pure curve degenerates completely while the golden fake grows exponentially on a
  fixed hyperbolic plane; the exponential rate of every curve is its zero width `max|Re s − ½|`.
-/
